A simple workflow — fetch a user, then their orders, then build a DTO — written in both styles.

**Imperative**
- Each line runs and blocks until done
- Errors thrown by `findById` or `findOrdersFor` propagate up
- All work happens on the calling thread

```java
public UserDto buildUserDto(String id) {
    User user = userRepo.findById(id);
    if (user == null) {
        throw new NotFoundException(id);
    }
    List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findByUserId(user.id());
    Stats stats = statsClient.fetch(user.id());
    return new UserDto(user, orders, stats);
}
```

**Reactive**
- The whole expression is a pipeline description
- Nothing runs until the framework subscribes (it does, for a controller return)
- `Mono.zip` lets the two downstream calls run in parallel
- An empty `findById` triggers `switchIfEmpty` — semantics for "not found" are explicit

```java
public Mono<UserDto> buildUserDto(String id) {
    return userRepo.findById(id)
        .switchIfEmpty(Mono.error(new NotFoundException(id)))
        .flatMap(user -> Mono.zip(
            orderRepo.findByUserId(user.id()).collectList(),
            statsClient.fetch(user.id()))
            .map(t -> new UserDto(user, t.getT1(), t.getT2())));
}
```

**Same logic on virtual threads**
- Imperative again — virtual threads make blocking I/O cheap
- Concurrency between `findByUserId` and `fetch` requires `CompletableFuture` or a small executor

```java
public UserDto buildUserDto(String id) {
    User user = userRepo.findById(id);
    if (user == null) throw new NotFoundException(id);
    return new UserDto(user,
        orderRepo.findByUserId(user.id()),
        statsClient.fetch(user.id()));
}
```

**What changes between the three:**
- Imperative blocking — simple, single thread, parallelism only with extra effort
- Imperative on virtual threads — same simplicity, but blocking is now cheap so concurrency scales
- Reactive — explicit parallelism via `Mono.zip`; explicit not-found via `switchIfEmpty`; the framework subscribes for you
